audit.natural_class:
Any SEMI join (IN-subquery, EXISTS, or a semi join from decorrelation) where the subquery returns far fewer rows than the join key has distinct values, typically a filtered or aggregated "top/hot keys" subquery. Examples:
1) SELECT u.name, e.ts FROM users u JOIN events e ON e.user_id = u.id WHERE u.id IN (SELECT user_id FROM purchases GROUP BY user_id HAVING sum(amount) > 10000);
2) SELECT * FROM orders_log o JOIN products p ON o.pid = p.pid WHERE o.session_id IN (SELECT session_id FROM fraud_flags WHERE flagged_at > now() - INTERVAL 1 DAY);
In both cases the right side is small compared with the key domain. The new estimate makes the semi-join result small, which can push it to the build side of a later join, as in q18. It does not apply to ANTI joins (NOT IN / NOT EXISTS) or to subqueries with large outputs.
